---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import TutorialQuickStart from '../../components/TutorialQuickStart.astro';
import { mediaStudioQuickStart } from '../../lib/tutorial-quick-start.mjs';
const hero = '/media-studio/studio-library-20261114.jpg';
const init = '/media-studio/init-media-20261114.jpg';
const sound = '/media-studio/studio-sound-20261114.jpg';
const storyboard = '/media-studio/studio-storyboard-20261114.jpg';
const audioAsset = '/media-studio/studio-audio-20261114.jpg';
const publicPage = '/media-studio/studio-public-20261114.jpg';

const chapters = [
  ['target', 'Understand the target app'],
  ['accounts', 'Accounts and the supported agent'],
  ['ready', 'Get Ready'],
  ['init', 'Choose Media Studio in Init'],
  ['handoff', 'Save and hand off the brief'],
  ['build', 'The agent builds and deploys'],
  ['enter', 'Enter through Composer'],
  ['make', 'Create and edit video and sound'],
  ['publish', 'Assemble, export and publish'],
  ['return', 'Return and change it'],
  ['pippa-demo', 'Make a clip and poster'],
  ['technical', 'Technical reference'],
];

const promptBrief = 'Build me a media studio for images, video and sound using my providers, with projects I can save and return to. Give it its own Cloudflare account resources, its own storage and its own release, and open it from my App Composer menu.';

const promptSound = 'A slow instrumental bed for a short studio film: warm analog pad, tape hiss, a patient four-note motif, brushed low percussion, no vocals, clean ending.';

const promptAgent = 'Use the saved Media Studio specification and the pinned source in this handoff. Create my own copy, apply every migration in order, set the secrets yourself without printing them, deploy, and read back the live revision. Then walk me through the one account step only I can do.';
---
<Base
  title="Build and run your own media studio with App Composer"
  description="A repeatable, illustrated tutorial: brief, Get Ready, Init, agent handoff, deployment, native access, real media work, assembly, publication and changing the same studio afterwards."
  shareImage="https://composer.loftwah.com/media-studio/media-studio-og-20261114.jpg"
  shareAlt="The Media Studio library with an assembled cut and generated sound."
  ogType="article"
  canonical="https://composer.loftwah.com/media-studio/tutorial"
>
<article class="tut">
  <header class="tut-head">
    <div class="tutorial-intro"><div class="tutorial-intro-copy">
    <p class="kicker">Media Studio · tutorial</p>
    <h1>Build and run your own media studio with App Composer</h1>
    <p class="deck">Make a studio for your own images, video and sound. This guide walks through the setup, your first creation and sharing the result, with help from your agent along the way. <a href="#target">Jump to the first step</a>.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <a class="button" href="/init?start=media-studio">Build your own studio</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://media.loftwah.com/gallery">Explore the gallery</a>
    </div>
    </div><figure class="tutorial-intro-art"><a href="https://media.loftwah.com/p/yFSN93LOFy1ja4j0"><img src="/media-studio/fighter-before-the-bell-20261114.webp" width="680" height="680" alt="Before the Bell: a Viking, a boxer and the Grim Reaper in a ring" /></a><figcaption>Before the Bell · a Media Studio variation of a Woodhouse original. <a href="#from-share">See the steps →</a></figcaption></figure></div>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>You need</dt><dd>A Cloudflare account on Workers Paid and a supported agent. Fal.ai generation and provider rendering also need a funded fal.ai key.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>You do not need</dt><dd>Docker, Wrangler, manual migrations or a database seed. The agent performs the technical work.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>Hosting, storage and paid generation are separate costs. The two-project snapshot recorded about US$1.66; separate tests added US$0.50 and roughly US$1.08. An earlier Paper &amp; Light run recorded about US$0.20. These are historical figures, not current prices or an invoice-confirmed bill.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Time</dt><dd>Allow an evening for your first build, including account setup and a first render. Later changes may take minutes.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <TutorialQuickStart guide={mediaStudioQuickStart} />
    <p><a href="/media-studio/direct">Direct scenes, sound and finished pieces with the creative field guide →</a></p>
    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Chapters">
      {chapters.map(([id, label], index) => (
        <a href={`#${id}`}>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')} {label}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>
    <p class="small">The walkthrough film follows these chapters. Timestamps below refer to <a href="#film">the illustrated film</a>.</p>
  </header>

  <section id="target" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">01 · Understand the target app</p>
    <h2>What you are building</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Decide what the studio is for and where its data will live.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Composer holds the brief; the studio app owns the projects and media.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A clear answer about ownership before any account work.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Your studio will have its own source, storage and release on Cloudflare. Your projects, job records and media stay with that app, and you can change it as your needs change.</p>
        <p>Composer helps you prepare the brief and open the studio. This build uses Cloudflare Workers AI for stills and fal.ai for video, sound and provider renders. You keep the source, data, media and publication pages.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>Check where the projects and media will live, and which provider charges for each request. In this build, projects live in the owner's Cloudflare account and media in its R2 bucket.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="accounts" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">02 · Accounts and the supported agent</p>
    <h2>The parts only you can do</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Create or sign in to Cloudflare, fund fal.ai, start your agent.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>The agent prepares the technical environment; it never creates accounts or spends for you.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A funded provider balance and an agent that can run commands.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>You control account creation, terms and spending. Start by:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Create or sign in to your Cloudflare account and confirm Workers Paid with Containers if you intend to use the Oyster route. The independent studio itself needs Workers Paid and Workers AI.</li>
          <li>Create a fal.ai account and add credit. Fal-backed video, sound and provider assembly bill there.</li>
          <li>Start a supported agent (for example a coding agent with terminal access) in an empty project directory and connect it to Composer's Get Ready.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Ask the agent to check the environment first; do not hand it the build brief yet:</p>
        <pre><code>Check this machine and my connected accounts with App Composer's Get Ready. Do not create or deploy anything yet. Tell me what is missing, then wait while I handle sign-in or billing.</code></pre>
        <p>You'll choose the brief and pinned source in Init, then hand them to your agent in chapter 05.</p>
        <p>You handle account creation, sign-in and spending decisions. Your agent can prepare the repository, configure the app, run migrations and deploy it. Keep secrets out of chat, screenshots and source files; enter them through the provider’s secret storage.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>You can see a funded fal.ai balance and your Cloudflare account has Workers AI available. No key has been pasted into chat, a prompt, a screenshot or a repository.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>No fal key yet?</strong> Uploads, the library, Workers AI stills, export and publication remain available. Fal-backed fills, cutout, upscale, video, sound and assembly need the studio’s own configured key; availability and funding are checked separately.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ready" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">03 · Get Ready</p>
    <h2>Check the machine before building</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Run Get Ready and read the report.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Checks the runtime, package manager, repository tools and accounts.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>Every check named, with a real pass or fail.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p><a href="/init">Init</a> includes Get Ready. It checks the local runtime, package manager, repository tools and accounts your agent needs, then reports anything missing.</p>
        <p>Read the result before continuing. If your Node version is unsupported, install a supported version and run the check again.</p>
        <p class="small">Get Ready does not create resources in your cloud account or require a subscription.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A readiness report showing which checks passed and what still needs attention.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>If the Node version check fails, install a supported version and run Get Ready again.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="init" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">04 · Choose Media Studio in Init</p>
    <h2>The brief</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Choose Media Studio, set the defaults, write the brief.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Validates the draft and builds a portable specification.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A live preview and a spec panel that updates with your choices.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open <a href="/init?start=media-studio">Init → Media Studio</a>. The recommendation is a preview until you select <strong>Use this starting point</strong>; then choose what the studio should make, the still and video models, the default frame and clip length, and the sound model and bed length. Name the project. None of this buys anything.</p>
        <p>Write the intent in your own words. The sentence used for this build:</p>
        <pre><code>{promptBrief}</code></pre>
        <p>The exported specification carries your choices. Revision 2 includes sound defaults, and revision 1 drafts still load.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>The Media Studio fieldset with default values, a live preview, and a "Portable specification" panel that updates as you change your choices.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>If the form rejects a value, it identifies the field. Correct it before exporting; unsupported model IDs are not accepted.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={init} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The Media Studio section of the Init form with name, focus, still and video models, default frame, clip length, sound model and sound length." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={init} aria-label="Open full-size Init form screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>Init's Media Studio section on the live page. The sound fields are part of revision 2 of the specification; revision 1 drafts still load.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="handoff" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">05 · Save and hand off the brief</p>
    <h2>Save the brief for your agent</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Save the draft, export the spec, copy the agent handoff.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Pins the source commit and lists the bindings, build plan and human-only actions.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A JSON file that round-trips, and a handoff naming an exact commit.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Save the draft, download the specification, and copy the agent handoff. The handoff pins the studio repository and commit, names the storage and secret bindings, states the build plan and separates the human-only actions from the technical ones.</p>
        <p>Now give the saved brief to the supported agent:</p>
        <pre><code>{promptAgent}</code></pre>
        <p>Keep the draft. Exporting does not publish anything, and importing a different specification later does not overwrite your saved draft until you choose to save it.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A JSON file you can re-import with identical values, and a handoff that names an exact forty-character commit — not "latest".</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>If the handoff shows a placeholder commit, the source pin is wrong. Stop; a moving target cannot be reproduced.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <p class="small">The <a href="/media-studio/media-studio-brief-20261114.json" download>example brief</a> from this run is available as a starting point.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="build" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">06 · The agent builds and deploys</p>
    <h2>Build and deploy the studio</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Approve the one account step only you can do.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Creates your copy, applies migrations in order, sets secrets, deploys and reads back the revision.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A live /healthz that returns the commit your agent shipped.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Your agent creates your copy of the studio, applies every migration in order, sets the secrets, deploys and reads back the live revision. On this run the release script refused a dirty tree, checked the pinned source, ran the focused tests, deployed, then compared <code>/healthz</code> to the commit it had shipped.</p>
        <p>The build hit two technical problems. The details are here if your agent needs them:</p>
        <details class="check"><summary>Migration and sign-in details</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Migrations are ordered and data-preserving.</strong> The audio release rebuilds two tables to widen a constraint; it copies every row and restores parent links in a second pass, because the production database enforces foreign keys.</li>
          <li><strong>The handoff expiry must match units.</strong> Composer mints a short-lived token; the studio compares milliseconds. A mismatch rejects a correct handoff as expired. Both sides now normalise.</li>
        </ul>
        </details>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p><code>/healthz</code> returning your exact commit, and the studio's login page offering the Composer route rather than only a passphrase.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>Deploy refused:</strong> read the message. It may identify a dirty tree, wrong branch, unpushed commit or a manifest that does not match the repository. Fix the reported problem and retry.</p>
        <p><strong>Foreign key error in a migration:</strong> do not delete data to get past it. Rebuild the parent first, then the children, then drop the old parent.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="enter" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">07 · Enter through Composer</p>
    <h2>No second password</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Open the studio from the Composer menu.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Exchanges the EmDash session for a short-lived studio handoff.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>Your projects, and private routes that return 401 signed out.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open the studio from the Composer menu or <a href="/media-studio/studio">Open your studio</a>. Composer passes your workspace, selected project and permitted actions to the studio through a short-lived handoff. The studio creates its own project-scoped session; your browser does not receive the studio owner passphrase or provider keys.</p>
        <p><strong>Email and passkeys:</strong> our selected sign-in improvement uses Cloudflare Access's native email codes and passkeys. Account binding and the live login rollout are still being completed; use this installation's existing sign-in method until the new options are enabled. The <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/docs/cloudflare-access-oyster.md">integration brief</a> explains setup and recovery. No Pocket ID server or Tailscale installation is required.</p>
        <p>Signing in is not the same as being granted a resource. Read, edit, generate, publish and admin are separate. The studio re-checks the current Composer grant on the server before a privileged action, so revoking a member stops the next dispatch even if their tab is still open. A signed-out request to a private route returns 401; a signed-in account without the grant gets a clear denied page, not a hidden menu item.</p>
        <p>You can also use the independent studio directly with its owner passphrase. The Composer route does not need that second sign-in.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>The studio's own header with the App Composer attribution, your projects, and a monthly allowance strip in the studio's currency, not Composer's.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>"This Composer handoff is not valid or has expired":</strong> reopen it from the menu rather than reusing an old URL; handoffs are deliberately short-lived.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={hero} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The studio library with an assembled cut, two waveform sound cards and three takes." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={hero} aria-label="Open full-size studio library screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>The library, opened from Composer: one assembled cut, two generated sound assets and the takes they came from.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="make" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">08 · Create and edit video and sound</p>
    <h2>Make your first asset</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Pick a model, write the direction, confirm the estimate.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>Sends the request and saves the result with its source and estimated cost.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>A saved still, clip or sound file. Audio shows a waveform and its duration.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Start by uploading an image, clip or sound file. The studio records the dimensions or duration and a hash with each file. Then open <strong>Create</strong>:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Pick a still model and a frame. Read the estimate before you confirm.</li>
          <li>For a real edit, choose <strong>Masked fill</strong>, paint the area to change, save the mask, then send the fill. <strong>Remove background</strong> and <strong>Upscale ×2</strong> are separate variants; the original stays.</li>
          <li>To animate, choose a saved still and one motion instruction. Video is asynchronous: you can close the browser.</li>
          <li>For sound, choose <strong>Make sound</strong>, pick Studio Score, Studio Voice or Studio Effect, write the direction and choose a length.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre><code>{promptSound}</code></pre>
        <p>The sound is saved as its own asset. Its duration, sample rate, channels and source are recorded, and its waveform comes from the decoded file.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A job in History, then a saved asset. For sound, a playable card with a waveform and its real duration.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>"This route needs a fal.ai key":</strong> the key is missing on the studio, not in your shell. Add it to the deployment's secrets.</p>
        <p><strong>Provider refuses for funding:</strong> check the saved job and the provider’s actual status before acting. If the outcome is unknown, keep its reservation and reconcile it; do not submit another paid request. A definitive funding refusal and any later new request still require the owner’s approval.</p>
        <p><strong>Save failed after the provider finished:</strong> use "Retry saving". It writes the retained bytes and does not generate again. Never mint a new request id to fix a save.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={sound} width="1440" height="900" alt="Create tab with Make sound selected, Studio Score, a written direction, a 17-second length and an estimate." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={sound} aria-label="Open full-size sound creation screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>One estimated request, with the length chosen to fit the cut.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={audioAsset} width="1440" height="900" alt="A saved narration asset with waveform, player, duration, sample rate, channels and hash." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={audioAsset} aria-label="Open full-size audio asset screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>Saved narration with its waveform and file details. You can publish it on its own page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="publish" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">09 · Assemble, export and publish</p>
    <h2>Assemble and share your cut</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Order the takes, add the bed, render, then publish deliberately.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>For a provider render, creates short-lived file links and saves a successful result to your bucket.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>An assembled MP4 with real duration and take count; a public page that loads signed out.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open <strong>Assemble</strong>. Add two to six saved takes in order, add up to four saved music, narration or effect tracks, set trim, gain and fades, and mix the soundtrack. The browser makes a new WAV mix and keeps the separate tracks and their settings. Save the storyboard to keep editing it later; earlier renders stay attached.</p>
        <p>The 9:16 option was designed to make a vertical opening and place your selected takes after it in order. During this build, fal.ai refused the compose request with a 403 permission error before reading the files. The studio kept the job and offers <strong>Reconcile</strong>, which checks that same request without sending another paid job. The 9:16 example in the article was finished locally from the saved takes and sound; it was not made with the blocked provider control.</p>
        <p>There is also a separate on-device MP4 renderer for saved storyboards in compatible browsers. It uses the saved mixed soundtrack and mutes the original clip audio. It does not repair the provider’s 9:16 compose route.</p>
        <p>When you are ready to share, check which action you need:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Publish / unpublish:</strong> create or withdraw a public page. Withdrawal stops new visits, but cannot recall a file someone already downloaded.</li>
          <li><strong>Show / hide:</strong> add or remove the card from discovery. Hiding it does not make the direct link private.</li>
          <li><strong>Make private:</strong> remove the media from public pages while keeping the originals in your library.</li>
          <li><strong>Delete:</strong> remove the file bytes and mark the publications that used them as deleted.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>An assembled MP4 with its dimensions, duration and take count; a public page that opens while signed out; private projects that remain accessible only to authorised people.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>A render that ends in "result unavailable":</strong> reconcile the job. It re-reads the same provider result; it does not dispatch a new one. If the provider returns 403 with a permission detail, stop and record the external block: fix the provider key or endpoint permission, then use Reconcile. Do not create a new request id.</p>
        <p><strong>The provider’s 9:16 render asks for a slate:</strong> use Make 9:16 cut to create the required saved slate image first. If a slate video already exists, the studio reuses it; otherwise it creates one. The separate on-device renderer has its own framing controls.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={storyboard} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Assemble tab with a saved storyboard, render controls and the assembled result." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={storyboard} aria-label="Open full-size storyboard screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>The storyboard and its render, in the same project that holds the takes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={publicPage} width="1440" height="900" alt="A public Media Studio page with the assembled cut, description, player and download." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={publicPage} aria-label="Open full-size public page screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>A published page, signed out. The example is live at <a href="https://media.loftwah.com/p/Na9FNUB_VhzZ7zA9">media.loftwah.com</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="return" class="step">
    <p class="kicker">10 · Return and change it</p>
    <h2>Make a change and check it</h2>
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>You</dt><dd>Ask for a change you actually want.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your agent or the studio</dt><dd>The same agent changes the same application and releases it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Look for</dt><dd>The new behaviour, with earlier projects, cuts and renders still there.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>In this build, the Assemble tab could not see saved storyboards, and audio beds were always thirty seconds long. The agent fixed both in the same application; the projects stayed in place.</p>
        <p>After that release: choose a bed length of 10 to 30 seconds, edit a storyboard in place, and see when a cut has changed since its last render. Earlier renders stay attached.</p>
        <p>To check the change, reopen the project, play the earlier cut and the new one, and compare their durations. Reopening reads the saved files without generating again.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>Two renders in one composition, 30.00 s and 17.02 s, both downloadable, with the takes unchanged.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>The change did not appear:</strong> check the live revision against the commit. A green build is not a deployment.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="from-share" class="step">
    <h2>Make a variation from Woodhouse Share</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find a still in <a href="https://media.loftwah.com/portfolio">Share artwork</a>. Read its source notes and check that you have reuse permission, then copy its asset ID.</li>
      <li>Open your Studio project. In Library, expand <strong>Import from Woodhouse Share</strong>, enter the ID and import the original. The source credit is saved with the file.</li>
      <li>Open the still and choose <strong>Make a variation</strong>. Select Studio Detail, describe one deliberate change and review the request estimate before submitting.</li>
      <li>Open the saved image from History. Compare it with the source, then use <strong>Publish page</strong> when the result is ready to share.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>See the <a href="/media-studio#from-share">Fighter source and result side by side</a>, then open the <a href="https://media.loftwah.com/p/yFSN93LOFy1ja4j0">published Studio output</a>.</p>
  </section>

